yield: for stockinpiggle pork spare ribs (200g ish)
uses:
  - crackling
ingredients:
  - item: pork spare ribs
    mass: 200
    basis: true
    note: ~3×3×15cm strips, skin removed
components:
  - name: braise
    ingredients:
      - item: white miso
        mass: 40
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
        note: red for more funk
      - item: maple syrup
        mass: 40
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
        note: dark/grade B preferred
      - item: soy or tamari
        mass: 8
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
      - item: ginger
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
        note: grated
      - item: garlic clove
        qty: 2
        unit: ea
        note: grated
      - item: rice vinegar or mirin
        mass: 5
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
        note: try 50:50
      - item: water or stock
        mass: 45
        note: 3-4 tbsp, to dilute
  - name: cook
    steps:
      - marinate a few hours (max ~12hr — miso enzymes turn meat mealy past that)
      - foil parcel, or a lidded stainless gastronorm — just a few tbsp of marinade per strip either way; braise, not swim (see notes on the vessel)
      - 140-150°C conventional for 90min–1h45 until yielding and wobbly; no springiness when pressed — check at 75 mins in foil, 60-65 in a gastronorm
      - open up; lift pork onto rack; pour braising liquid into saucepan
      - reduce braising liquid to thick, glossy, mahogany glaze
      - 'split glaze: ~2/3 cooking, ~1/3 reserve for serving'
      - brush cooking glaze onto pork; blast 200-220°C fan-forced (on rack not back in foil) for 5-10 mins watching closely - second coat halfway through for thicker lacquer; blowtorch works great here
      - rest, then slice perpendicular into 1.5-2cm pieces
  - name: cook — over coals (variant)
    steps:
      - braise exactly as above — the parcel is steam, not smoke, and gains nothing from the fire; oven, full stop
      - build the fire well ahead and let it burn down to a settled coal bed — fresh flame scorches this glaze in seconds
      - if the bed height adjusts, set it so the hand test at grate level reads 4-5 secs; otherwise rake the coals to one side for a cooler half
      - reduce and split the glaze as above, but hold back ~half rather than a third — fire flattens the bright top notes harder than an oven does
      - brush the cooking glaze on and set it at that height, upturned bowl over the top, 3-4 mins to tack up
      - second coat, then drop the bed to a 2-3 sec height for colour — 30-60 secs a face, turning; on a fixed grate instead take 20-30 sec passes over the edge of the coals, never parked over live heat
      - pull the moment the edges bubble and darken; blistered black spots are good, a uniformly carbonised face is not
      - rest and slice as above, serving glaze at the plate
    notes: |
      The blast at the end is the only part of this recipe that wants fire, and it's the part
      coals genuinely do better — the fat cap picks up a rendered, faintly smoky crispness a
      fan oven won't give you, plus a bit of drip-flare character.

      Sugar is the whole constraint. Reduced maple and miso is high-sugar and high-protein, and
      it starts burning around 160-170°C surface temp — far below what a live coal bed gives
      you directly. Hence setting the glaze at a distance first, where it can't scorch, and
      only then taking brief closer passes for colour. Same reasoning as brisket variant B
      keeping dry sugar away from the drum entirely.

      If the bed height adjusts, use that rather than banking. Radiant heat falls off roughly
      with the square of the distance, so height is a smooth continuous control where banking
      is two settings — and you can leave the strips sitting at the high setting for minutes
      instead of doing 20-second dashes. Moving the bed also beats handling sticky glazed pork
      repeatedly. Keep sliding sideways off the coals as the instant bail-out if a face darkens
      faster than you want to crank for.

      Keep the smoke to a kiss. This is a ten-minute exposure at most — the braise flavour is
      delicate and an afternoon of smoke would bury it. The no-lid drum's thin smoke deposit
      works in your favour here for once.

  - name: serving glaze
    steps:
      - 'loosen reserved glaze: squeeze of lime or few drops rice vinegar'
      - half-tsp fresh grated ginger stirred in off heat
      - thin with a splash of water if needed
      - tiny dab of fresh miso if the flavour has gone flat in the reduction
      - spoon over sliced pork at the plate
  - name: plating
    steps:
      - slices slightly overlapping, cut-side up to show the fat/meat layers
      - serving glaze drizzled over
      - thinly sliced spring onion or toasted sesame seeds to finish
notes: |
  [ the vessel — foil vs gastronorm ]
  A lidded stainless gastronorm is the better container. The marinade is both salty and acidic
  (miso, soy, vinegar), which attacks aluminium over 90 mins at 150°C — grey pitting inside the
  parcel and a faint metallic edge. Stainless doesn't care.

  Use the smallest pan the strips fit in — a 1/6 or 1/9 deep, not a 1/3 sitting mostly empty.
  Part of why foil works is that it's tight: minimal headspace means the few tbsp of liquid
  saturate the air straight away and stay there. A large pan is a lot of cold air to fill and
  the liquid goes to the lid instead. A GN lid rests rather than seals, so if it rattles, sit a
  sheet of baking paper under it (paper, not foil — same reason as above) or crimp foil over the
  rim outside the lid. Condensate should drip back in, not escape.

  Same 140-150°C either way — the target is steam at ~100°C in the pan and the oven temp only
  sets how fast you get there. Stainless conducts better than a pouch and the base sits in real
  contact with the rack, so it runs a little faster and the underside gets actual pan contact
  and browns rather than just steaming, which is a plus. Don't preheat the pan; strips go in
  cold with the marinade.

  [ why so little liquid in the parcel ]
  The braising liquid that comes out should be a few tablespoons of intense, slightly fatty,
  mahogany-coloured liquid — not a cup of pale water. A lot of liquid dilutes the pork's own
  rendered fat and gelatin into the surrounding water rather than concentrating it in the glaze.
  The small amount also keeps equilibrium in the parcel: instead of leaching flavour out of
  the meat (the direction of stock-making), the seasoning concentrates around it.

  The foil traps steam that circulates at ~100°C, cooking evenly from all sides. Bottom of the
  meat braises and absorbs flavour; top half cooks in steam. Rendered fat stays in the pool
  rather than floating on a cup of water that has to be skimmed.

  [ the two-glaze principle ]
  Glaze brushed on before the high-heat blast caramelises and deepens — great for colour and
  lacquer, but the bright top notes (ginger zing, vinegar tang, fresh miso funk) cook out.
  Reserved glaze keeps those intact. Layering them gives a caramelised surface hit first, then
  brighter aromatic flavour as you chew. Always hold some back.

  [ miso & maple notes ]
  White miso: sweeter, milder — good starting point. Red miso: funkier, more assertive.
  Dark/grade B maple brings woody depth; light maple reads as just sweet. Miso is already salty
  — taste before adding more soy or salt. Proteases in miso tenderise aggressively, so don't
  push the marinade past ~12hr.

  [ on the skin ]
  If not dried overnight, remove skin and do crackling separately (see crackling recipe).
  A wet, glazed skin under a high-heat ramp will go leathery rather than crackled.
